# App improvements
Order matters. Do not start fan/blower control, Apple Watch, or accounts yet.

## Table stakes other BBQ apps already have
- [ ] Alerts when the phone leaves the Pi's Wi-Fi (ntfy, Telegram, or the
  outbound relay from phase 3). This is the feature people pay FireBoard
  and Meater for.
- [ ] Name probes 3–6 and give each an independent target and ETA. Slots exist
  in state.js but are unused. A brisket needs a flat and a point, not one
  "Meat" probe.
- [DONE] Apply the °F/°C setting (Settings > Appearance). Data stays in °C;
  display, target inputs, charts, notifications and recipe text convert.
- [DONE] Rest timer when the core hits the final phase target, plus a carryover
  estimate from the last 10 minutes of slope (capped at 8°C). Length adjustable
  in 5 min steps; notification when the rest ends.
- [DONE] Lid openings are flagged on the cook and history charts. Tap the
  flag to say why it was opened. Wrap / spritz / sauce markers are still open.
- [DONE] Share a cook card with friends: name, date, duration, rating, peaks
  and the first photo. Notes and "next time" stay private. CSV export is
  still open.

## What would make this app different
- [DONE] Vent guidance: dashboard and notifications say what to do with the
  bottom vent and top daisy wheel, including leaving them alone while the
  lid is open.
- [DONE] Cook like this: history loads that cook's targets and phases into a
  new cook, and the live chart draws the previous dome and core as dashed lines.
- [ ] Stall advice tied to the recipe: wrap now, or leave it because the bark
  is not set. One decision, not a chatbot.
- [ ] Danger-zone time: how long the core sat between 5°C and 60°C.

# Project plan: broader availability + monetization
Decisions: audience = hobbyist community (tens-hundreds). Start self-hosted
(each user has their own Pi), design so a hybrid model (hosted accounts + Pi
connects out) can be added later. Monetize via paid cloud features (sync,
remote alerts, backup) and paid AI coaching; core app stays free.

